    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d303">

                <pb facs="0440" n="428" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg428"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">195.7 <hi rendition="#italic">Richard
                    Hovey</hi>/1–3145: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Huddle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-12">February 12,
                        1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>679. Request Swiss Foreign Office to deliver following message verbatim to
                    Japanese Government in reference to Department’s telegrams 2043, June 14, 1944
                    and Legation’s 8041, December 8, 1944:<anchor n="8" xml:id="d303-f1"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Concerning United States protest regarding the Japanese attacks upon
                            survivors of torpedoed <hi rendition="#italic">Richard Hovey</hi>, the
                            United States Government notes that the Japanese Government, after a
                            reported thorough examination of the facts involved, states that no
                            action has been revealed corresponding to that set forth in the protest
                            under reference.</p>
                        <p>“The evidence stated in the protest of the United States Government was
                            specific. Statements of survivors who witnessed the attack are of
                            unchallenged validity. The United States Government, therefore, reserves
                            all rights in the matter.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <pb facs="0441" n="429" xml:id="pg429"/>
                <p>The Swiss Minister at Tokyo should be requested to telegraph date of delivery to
                    Japanese Government.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d303-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d303-f1" n="8" xml:id="d303-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1944v05#pg_1175">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1944, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, pp. 1175</ref> and <ref
                        target="frus1944v05#pg_1176">1176</ref>, respectively.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d303-f2" n="9" xml:id="d303-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 1320, March 1, the Minister in Switzerland reported delivery of the
                    message on February 21 (195.7 <hi rendition="#italic">Richard
                    Hovey</hi>/3–145).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
